WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.129
In this lesson we work with dates and time.

2
00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:06.768
On this page there is a date slicer built from the calendar.

3
00:00:06.768 --> 00:00:09.800
I format it so the dates are easy to read.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.789
When I change the start and end dates, every visual on the page updates

5
00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:18.500
to show only that period.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.280
The card on the right shows the current date and time, using the NOW function.

7
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.800
TODAY works the same way, but returns only the date.

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.300
Here in Model view we see the tables we will use.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.300
The Customers table holds the customer details.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.822
And the Orders table has three important dates: the order date, the required date,

11
00:01:02.822 --> 00:01:04.200
and the shipped date.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.150
On the report page, a card uses NOW to show when the report was refreshed.

13
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:20.300
Our goal now is to calculate the number of days between two dates with DATEDIFF.

14
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.568
I create a new measure called Date Interval, and start typing DATEDIFF.

15
00:01:52.568 --> 00:01:57.897
It needs three arguments: the first date, the second date, and the unit, such

16
00:01:57.897 --> 00:01:59.800
as DAY, MONTH, or YEAR.

17
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.409
If we leave out an argument, Power BI warns us: too few arguments were

18
00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:16.568
passed to DATEDIFF.

19
00:02:16.568 --> 00:02:18.500
So we provide all three.

20
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.760
Because we want the difference for each order, we will calculate it row by

21
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.180
row in the Orders table, between the order date and the required date.

22
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.600
This shows how much time each order had, and which orders were late.

